※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4563

※ A `&mut` is the only reference to what it names for as long as it lives, and
※ a `&` may share with other `&`s.  That is what makes a reference worth having:
※ whoever holds a `&mut` knows nothing else can change the place under it, and
※ whoever holds a `&` knows nothing can change it at all.  Two references where
※ either may write would give up both promises at once, so the second is
※ refused and a note points at the first.
※
※ How long the first one lives is what decides whether there is a clash.  A
※ reference a name was bound to lives as long as that name's scope; one nothing
※ bound -- handed straight to a call -- is gone when the statement is, which is
※ why `bump(&mut n); bump(&mut n)` is two turns and not two references at once.
※ That is the lexical rule, and it is Rust's before non-lexical lifetimes: a
※ borrow there now ends at its last use, which reads more programs but needs a
※ liveness analysis to say where a reference stops existing.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let n: mut u8 = 1u8
    let r: &mut u8 = &mut n
    let second: &mut u8 = &mut n
    r⌖ ← 2u8
    second⌖ ← 3u8
    0u6
